INTRODUCTION
============
This program  was originally  a COLOR64  BBS game  written by Steve Wagy and
Terry Murphy.  It has been re-written to run on an Amiga  (kickstart 1.2 and
up) running  Paragon BBS  version 2.0 or higher.  The game employs some ANSI
graphics and ANSI color  - keep  this in  mind when  installing the  game so
people know which terminal type they need to use.

But, I  have seen the light and found DLG Pro an overall better BBS program.
So, I ported the game over  to DLG.   Since  it wasn't  a complete re-write,
some limitations  due exist.   It is not completely CLI based (though, could
easily  be  done),  so  it  only  runs  under   DLG  for   the  time  being.
Unfortunately, it requires Kickstart 2.04 or higher (works under 3.0).  And,
currently only eight people can simultaneously play the game with  a maximum
of 30 total players.

I have  a huge  list of  ideas to add to the game, but I'm still looking for
people to give me feedback on this version.



FILES NEEDED
============
SM.CLIENT      Main program - run by each player.

SM.SERVER      Sub-program - controls most file handling and lets
               all SM.CLIENT programs talk to each other for
               multi-player game.

SM.DATA        Contains data for the companies.
               File structure:
                         Line 1: Number of companies in the file
                         Line 2: Name of the first company
                         Line 3: Shares available
                         Line 4: Initial Price per Share
                         Line 5: Trend Value
                         Line 6: Risk Value
                         Line 7: Name of the second company
                         Line 8: etc, etc, etc.

                         Trend Value -  Higher the number, the
                                        greater the gain/loss
                                        will be when the stock is
                                        updated.

                         Risk Value -   Higher the number, the
                                        riskier the stock will
                                        become.  This controls
                                        whether a stock could
                                        have a negative gain.

SM.MENU        Menu File. (Not needed for set-up, BUT RECOMMENDED)

SM.WINNER      Past winner file. Each time someone wins, their
               name is placed into this file. (Not needed for
               set-up, BUT RECOMMENDED)

SM.TITLE       Title file. (Not needed for set-up, BUT
               RECOMMENDED)

SM.HELP        On-line game instructions. (Not needed for set-up,
               BUT RECOMMENDED)

SM.DOC         Your reading this file now.



OTHER FILES USED (NOT NEEDED UPON INITIAL SET-UP)
=================================================
The files listed below are generated by the game.

SM.NEWS1       Contains today's news.

SM.NEWS2       Contains previous playing day news.

SM.STOCKS      Contains current stock information.

SM.PLAYERS     Contains current player information.

SM.LINE        Contains current stock tip and the name of the last player.



FILES SUPPLIED IN THE LHA
=========================
If any of the following files are missing, or any new files  have been added
- DO NOT  USE THE  GAME - get another copy from some other source.  (See BUG
REPORTS).

SM.CLIENT, SM.SERVER, SM.MENU,  SM.WINNER,  SM.TITLE,  SM.DOC,  SM.HELP, and
SM.DATA



INSTALLING THE GAME
===================
In the DOORS: directory, create a sub-directory named SM_V2.  Then, copy all
the  NEEDED  files  (SM.CLIENT,  SM.SERVER,  SM.DATA,   SM.MENU,  SM.WINNER,
SM.TITLE, SM.HELP) into that sub-directory.

After the general set-up (creating the sub-directory, copying the files, and
adding a menu entry), nothing else is involved  in running  the game (unless
you want  to change the SM.TITLE, SM.WINNER, SM.MENU, and SM.DATA files.  In
the case of SM.DATA, the file structure was listed above.   The  other files
are standard  text.   Use a text editor such as DME, ED, CED, QED, LSE, etc.
to edit any of  these files.)   The  game is  self-running to  reduce system
overhead and to reduce the time spent running and up keeping the game.


SETTING UP THE GAME
===================
Create a menu entry.

Executable: (path/name)  doors:sm_v2/sm.client username "%name" port "%port"
Type:                    Executable
Load Type:               Overlay
CLI Mode:                No
Priority:                Default

The other settings are up to you.



OPERATION
=========
When the game is first run (or anytime a new game occurs), the program reads
in the SM.DATA file and randomly picks 10 companies from that file.   (There
MUST BE  AT LEAST  10 companies in the SM.DATA file.  There's also a maximum
limit of 40 companies for the SM.DATA file.)

If at sometime a  company  goes  bankrupt,  the  program  will  replace that
company after  three (3)  PLAYING DAYS.   It  will randomly choose a company
from the SM.DATA file, making sure  it doesn't  pick a  company that already
exists. (NOTE:  The company  that went bankrupt could be replaced by itself.
This is always the case when  only 10  companies are  in the  SM.DATA file.)
Also, if  a player  is inactive  for ten  (10) playing  days, that person is
removed.

A "PLAYING DAY" is considered any day someone actually plays the game.   So,
if  someone  plays  the  game  on  Monday  and  no  one  plays on Tuesday or
Wednesday, only Monday is consider a playing day.



HELPFUL TIPS
============
Game instructions are located in the SM.HELP file.

To close the Stock Market game,  simply rename  or delete  the SM.DATA file.
The game  will then be CLOSED until the SM.DATA file is restored.  (Removing
the game from the menu may sometimes be a better choice.)

To manually restart a game, just delete the SM.STOCKS file.   The  next time
someone plays Stock Market, a new game is initiated.



MISCELLANEOUS
=============
There is  no support  within the  program to delete a player, edit a player,
replace a company, or edit a company in an active game.  The only  way to do
this is  manually edit  the SM.STOCKS  or SM.PLAYERS  file; however, this is
highly discouraged.  Invalid data may disrupt the current game or even cause
system problems.  No information will be on the structure on these files.



